--- private-mixtral-bm32 ---
  closes: ALPHA AS A RATIO OF TWO MEASURED SLOPES, with no assumed bandwidth and no fitted intercept anywhere in it, which is the one thing the 2026-09-10 reading says this apparatus has never had. Every alpha in this study divides a time by an ASSUMED rate or by an extrapolation back to n=0, and that is why the same six cells give 0.9794 under one parameterisation and 0.5977 under another against a quoted sd of 0.0113. This arm runs the ladder THREE TIMES at one geometry, with copy c of expert e at expert slot e x n_decl + c (EXPERT-FIRST): SHARED (E x n_decl experts declared at EVERY tread, every M-tile routed to copy 0), PRIVATE (the same declaration and tensors, M-tile j routed to copy j, so reuse is impossible BY CONSTRUCTION and the slope IS the alpha=1 reference), and NATIVE (the study's own call, E declared over a strided view of copy 0: the same bytes at the same addresses, which is the control V5 reads to tie the matched ratio to the path the study fits). n_decl is the deepest tread's copy count padded until E x n_decl clears vLLM's small-batch expert bound, which is 9 copies and 72 slots at the booked ladder: 6 read, 3 filled and never read. SHARED and PRIVATE share the sorted-id buffer, the launch grid, the dead launches and the tile ORDER, and differ only in the addresses tiles read; an earlier copy-first layout that declared E x n at tread n broke both, adding dead launches to the private slope alone and running its tiles in a different order, and order is the lever this study measured moving the per-M-tile cost by 30-48%. V7 requires the two arms' clocks to agree at every tread. AND THE ALIGNMENT KERNEL IS SEPARATED, THREE WAYS: vLLM's moe_align_block_size switches kernel at ids < 1024 and experts <= 64 (cited to v0.27.1's csrc/libtorch_stable/moe/moe_align_sum_kernels.cu), which for mixtral at BM=32 falls between treads 3 and 4, inside the fit; the ratio arms declare 9 copies (72 experts) so both take one kernel throughout and the census REFUSES a plan where they do not; a probe times the alignment op alone along the ladder, once per arm (NATIVE's declaration, and SHARED's and PRIVATE's id sets at the ratio arms'), before a weight is allocated, and V8 refuses the design on the MEASUREMENT if the ratio arms' series carries a step worth more than 0.01 of the ratio (the step is judged at the Student-t quantile of its own standard error, widened for the fact that its split was chosen by minimum residual, and bounded over both ratio arms with no premise that the step is common), skipping the sweep on a FAIL ONLY: an UNKNOWN is a statement about the probe, not the design, so the ladder still runs and the page latches INVALID with every other gate's number on it. On a host-bound probe, which an H200 is expected to give, NATIVE's own switch at the census tread is the positive control: resolved there, the probe has shown it sees a kernel switch through the host cost and a flat ratio series earns PASS; and NATIVE, which keeps the study's E=8 and so its switch, has its difference from SHARED fitted with a step term, so V5 reads the declaration's per-tile cost with the step out and the step is printed with an interval. Nothing here bounds a cost paid by READING the copies, and the plan page says so. slope(shared)/slope(private) is alpha as a traffic fraction with no assumed RATE in it, and it is a traffic fraction on the further assumption that both arms deliver the same bytes per second, which the page registers rather than hides. NO KERNEL WAS WRITTEN: vLLM fused_moe reads its expert index PER M-TILE and addresses experts through stride(0), so a private copy per tile is a relabelling of topk_ids plus a wider w1/w2. WHAT IT SETTLES, either way: a ratio in ALPHA_BAND confirms the refit as a traffic fraction; a ratio near 1.0 says the whole weight set is re-read and the model SHAPE was right where its coefficient was not identifiable; a ratio under 0.35 says most of the per-M-tile cost is issue and latency, the traffic model is the wrong KIND of model, and the study's negative result becomes a positive one. The arm PRINTS which, from a partition registered before the run. BY-PRODUCT: slope(private) read as a rate is the delivered weight-read bandwidth of the kernel under test, which is the denominator every published w is divided by, measured. IT RUNS AFTER THE PIN PROBES because it forces its tile through the same override hook they validate, and AFTER calibrate because C2 and V4 are scored against this card own ridge and triad. BM=32 AND NOT 64, and that is the design, not taste: at 64 a world of alpha=0.10 puts the shared ladder over 95% of the roof from tread 3, so V4 would void the page in the one outcome worth the most, and the script REFUSES that configuration at plan time with its discrimination floor printed.
